Council decision 11 146 is published at "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/static/dab/decisions/council-decisions/11-146.pdf".
the text of Council decision 11 146 is at "sources/council/11-146.txt".
Council decision m 11 12 is published at "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/static/dab/decisions/council-decisions/m_11_12.pdf".
the text of Council decision m 11 12 is at "sources/council/m_11_12.txt".
Council decision couriermed is published at "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/static/dab/decisions/council-decisions/couriermed.pdf".
the text of Council decision couriermed is at "sources/council/couriermed.txt".
Council decision kohlls pharmacy is published at "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/static/dab/decisions/council-decisions/kohlls_pharmacy.pdf".
the text of Council decision kohlls pharmacy is at "sources/council/kohlls_pharmacy.txt".
Council decision m 11 1177 is published at "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/static/dab/decisions/council-decisions/m_11_1177.pdf".
the text of Council decision m 11 1177 is at "sources/council/m_11_1177.txt".
Council decision m 15 582 is published at "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/static/dab/decisions/council-decisions/m-15-582.pdf".
the text of Council decision m 15 582 is at "sources/council/m-15-582.txt".

query pay is:
    which claim is payable.

query rn is:
    which claim is reasonable and necessary.

query stage is:
    the query fails at which section.

% M-11-146, Gordian Medical d/b/a American Medical Technologies (16
% September 2011). Surgical dressings furnished from April 2008 to December
% 2009 to residents of long-term care facilities — foam (A6209-A6212),
% collagen (A6021), alginate (A6196), hydrogel (A6231, A6242, A6248), gauze
% roll (A6446), tape (A4452): a universe of 1147 claims, 49 sampled (one
% remanded). For each claim the record holds the facility admission record,
% the supplier's "Nursing Facility Patient Wound Care Order Sheet" signed and
% dated by the physician, the supplier's wound evaluation form dated on or a
% few days before the date of service, and an invoice or proof of delivery.
% Council: not covered — the forms give "at most, monthly snapshots": no
% primary, daily or weekly documentation of the clinical course of the
% wounds substantiates the types and quantities (LCDs L11449, L11460,
% L11471; PIM ch. 5 §5.7), a physician's form not being conclusive; the
% LCDs' foam criteria (full thickness, moderate to heavy exudate, up to three
% changes a week as a primary dressing) are owed substantial deference, and
% the Council cannot review an LCD's validity. The ALJ's zero percent
% extrapolated to the universe; the supplier liable (section 1879), recovery
% not waived (section 1870).
% The Council decides no claim individually: the foam claim of K.A., the
% example the decision gives, stands for the sample (its code is not stated:
% A6209, the first foam code the decision lists, is assumed). Left out: the
% other 48 sample claims and the other categories, known only through the
% general holding; R.B.'s foam claim, withdrawn.
% Model: not reasonable and necessary — agrees: the decision states nothing
% of the wound that the foam criteria read (its thickness, its exudate), nor
% the quantity or the months supplied. Not payable either: the date of
% service, the order's date and elements and the evaluation's date (all in
% the decision's Attachment A) are not stated, and the "invoice or proof of
% delivery" is not said to be a proof of delivery. In today's words the
% Council's demand for weekly documentation is the Article's weekly
% evaluation of a nursing facility resident's wound (the rule
% dressing_evaluation_interval), which the model would apply once the dates
% are known; a long-term care facility is read as a nursing facility.
% Candidate templates: "the medical record of *a beneficiary* documents the
% clinical course of *a wound*" (contemporaneous documentation, beyond the
% supplier's forms, of the wound's worsening or improvement), "*a document*
% was generated by the supplier".
scenario m_11_146 is, as stated in Council decision 11 146:
    claim 1 is for the foam dressings,
        confer "the claim for foam dressings furnished to beneficiary K.A.".
    the HCPCS code of the foam dressings is "A6209",
        confer "foam dressings (A6209, A6210, A6211, A6212)".
    the foam dressings is furnished to KA,
        confer "the claim for foam dressings furnished to beneficiary K.A.".
    the use of the foam dressings is primary dressing,
        confer "Medicare does not cover the foam dressings furnished by the appellant as primary dressings".
    the foam dressings is applied to the wound of KA,
        confer "no medical documentation on the ongoing progress of the wound has been provided".
    the wound of KA is a wound of KA,
        confer "no medical documentation on the ongoing progress of the wound has been provided".
    the residence of KA is nursing facility,
        confer "various surgical dressings it furnished to residents of long-term care facilities".
    the order for the foam dressings is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "signed and dated by the beneficiary’s treating physician".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at question"].

% M-11-12, Gordian Medical d/b/a American Medical Technologies (8 August
% 2011). The same supplier and forms as M-11-146: dressings furnished from
% December 2007 to January 2009 to residents of long-term care facilities —
% foam (A6209, A6210, A6212), impregnated gauze (A6222), collagen (A6021),
% specialty absorptive (A6253, which the decision lists with the conforming
% bandage A6446, and A6210, a foam code), alginate (A6197), tape (A4452),
% hydrogel (A6242, A6248), transparent film (A6257), composite (A6200,
% A6201, A6203): a universe of 812 claims, 50 sampled. The ALJ covered all
% of A.A.'s dressings and part of L.B.'s, J.F.'s, A.S.'s and M.S.'s, and
% extrapolated 11.6 percent to the universe.
% Council: reverses — none covered: (1) the documentation, as in M-11-146,
% does not substantiate the types and quantities, not even up to the LCDs'
% frequency limits the ALJ allowed; (2) the borderless composite dressings
% billed under A6200 and A6201 are not covered: CMS ended their coverage on
% 1 July 2007 (Transmittal 1388) and deleted the codes from the 2008 fee
% schedule, and neither an ALJ nor the Council has authority to review a
% HCPCS definition, the invalidation of a code or its deletion from the fee
% schedule (42 CFR 405.926), so the ALJ's deference to the Policy Articles
% that called the codes "invalid for claim submission" was harmless.
% Transcribed: A.A.'s claim, the one the ALJ covered in full (its items and
% code are not stated: foam A6209 is assumed, as in M-11-146), and the two
% borderless composite claims, with the codes billed (their beneficiaries,
% sample exhibits 8, 9, 16 and 17, are not named, and no fact says whom
% each was furnished to). Left out: the partial coverage of L.B., J.F., A.S.
% and M.S., and the 45 claims the ALJ denied (Y.B.'s is the decision's
% example), known only through the general holding; the beneficiary in a
% skilled nursing facility who had exhausted his Part A days.
% Model: none reasonable and necessary — agrees. A.A.: the wound, the
% quantity and the months are not stated. A6200 and A6201: no code of
% today's HCPCS — the surgical dressings policy lists only the bordered
% composite dressings A6203-A6205 — so the claims are outside the model's
% scope: like the Council, the model takes the code set as given and
% decides nothing for a code no policy lists. The stage of the whole is
% A.A.'s (question): the scope goal holds for claim 1, so applicability is
% not blamed for claims 2 and 3. Candidate template: "*a code* was deleted
% from the fee schedule on *a date*" (a code of the claim's day no longer in
% the code set).
scenario m_11_12 is, as stated in Council decision m 11 12:
    claim 1 is for the dressings of AA,
        confer "the ALJ granted coverage for all of the surgical dressings furnished to an individual beneficiary".
    the HCPCS code of the dressings of AA is "A6209",
        confer "foam dressings (A6209, A6210, A6212)".
    the dressings of AA is furnished to AA,
        confer "beneficiary A.A., sample number 1".
    the residence of AA is nursing facility,
        confer "various surgical dressings it furnished to residents of long-term care facilities".
    the order for the dressings of AA is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "signed and dated by the beneficiary’s treating physician".
    claim 2 is for the borderless composite dressings,
        confer "certain claims for borderless composite surgical dressings claimed under codes A6200 and A6201".
    the HCPCS code of the borderless composite dressings is "A6200",
        confer "certain claims for borderless composite surgical dressings claimed under codes A6200 and A6201".
    claim 3 is for the second borderless composite dressings,
        confer "certain claims for borderless composite surgical dressings claimed under codes A6200 and A6201".
    the HCPCS code of the second borderless composite dressings is "A6201",
        confer "certain claims for borderless composite surgical dressings claimed under codes A6200 and A6201".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at question"].

% CourierMed (24 November 2009, own-motion review). The CD-1000, a composite
% dressing and pouch under a removable tarp, billed under A6204 (composite,
% pad more than 16 and at most 48 square inches, adhesive border): 271
% claims for 135 beneficiaries with end-stage renal disease between 1
% October 2007 and 2 September 2008, all dialysed through a catheter, in a
% facility or at home under Method I; twelve dressings a month on the
% physicians' prescriptions, placed over the catheter dressing so that the
% beneficiary could shower. The ALJ covered them under LCD L11449 as
% reasonable and necessary, within its limit, used at home and not with the
% dialysis.
% Council: not separately payable — dressings used with dialysis access
% catheters are covered under the end-stage renal disease benefit and
% included in the composite rate paid to the dialysis facility (Policy
% Article A24114; Benefit Policy Manual ch. 11 §30 and §50.6.1.2), whether
% primary or secondary, during dialysis or after; not a
% reasonable-and-necessary denial, so section 1879 does not apply. The QIC's
% other grounds (inpatients, nursing facility residents, home health, other
% primary insurers) are not addressed. One beneficiary stands for the 135;
% no date of service is given.
% Model: not reasonable and necessary — agrees on the outcome only: the
% composite dressing criterion (a moderately to highly exudative wound)
% reads an exudate the decision does not state; the quantity (12 a month,
% three changes a week) is within the usual. DISAGREES on the ground: the
% Council's criterion is in today's texts and not in the model — Policy
% Article A54563: "Dressings used with dialysis access catheters (covered
% under the end stage renal disease benefit) are included in the composite
% rate (outpatient facility dialysis) or payment cap (method 1 home
% dialysis) paid to the dialysis provider", and LCD L33831: "Dressings over
% a percutaneous catheter or tube ... are covered as long as the catheter or
% tube remains in place"; surgical_dressings.le reads no catheter, so with
% an exudate stated the model would find reasonable and necessary a dressing
% the Council finds paid under another benefit. Not payable either: the exit
% site is not stated to be surgical or debrided (a qualifying wound), the
% dates are not stated. Candidate templates: "*a wound* is the exit site of
% a dialysis access catheter", "the dialysis payment method of *a
% beneficiary* is *a method*" (facility, Method I, Method II), "*a
% catheter* remains in place on *a date*".
scenario couriermed is, as stated in Council decision couriermed:
    claim 1 is for the composite dressings,
        confer "a surgical dressing which it markets under the name".
    the HCPCS code of the composite dressings is "A6204",
        confer "code A6204, thereby defining the item as".
    the composite dressings is furnished to the beneficiary,
        confer "which it had provided to 135 beneficiaries with end-stage renal disease (ESRD)".
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months,
        confer "the appellant had provided twelve dressings per month to each of the beneficiaries".
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 12,
        confer "the appellant had provided twelve dressings per month to each of the beneficiaries".
    the use of the composite dressings is secondary dressing,
        confer "The CD-1000 dressings at issue were generally placed over other dressings to protect the catheter site from moisture".
    the composite dressings is applied to the catheter exit site,
        confer "houses the catheter and covers the exit wound site".
    the catheter exit site is a wound of the beneficiary,
        confer "All of the patients at issue in this case were receiving dialysis services via a catheter".
    the medical record of the beneficiary documents end stage renal disease,
        confer "beneficiaries with end-stage renal disease (ESRD)".
    the residence of the beneficiary is private residence,
        according to the ALJ,
        confer "for use at home to maintain the wound area of the catheter".
    the order for the composite dressings is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "pursuant to prescriptions issued by treating physicians".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at question"].

% Kohll's Pharmacy & Homecare (30 September 2009). Zinc paste impregnated
% bandages (A6456) for Unna boots, provided 30 June 2008: billed as 240
% units, amended to 160 (16 packages of 10). On 24 June 2008 the physician
% found the venous stasis ulcers healed but multiple small punctate ulcers
% and edema keeping them from healing, and prescribed Unna boots twice a
% week for a month to both legs; a month later the ulcers had healed.
% Council: not covered — the Policy Article A23903 covers surgical dressings
% only for a wound caused or treated by a surgical procedure or after
% debridement, and the punctate ulcers were neither (which "is not to say
% that the use of these dressings ... was not medically appropriate"); the
% physician's records, excluded by the ALJ, are admitted for good cause.
% For liability the denial is one of reasonableness and necessity (PIM ch. 3
% §3.4.1.2.E): the supplier liable, the beneficiary not.
% Model: not reasonable and necessary — agrees on the outcome, on the ALJ's
% ground (the type and quantity not supported) rather than the Council's:
% the zinc paste criterion (a venous leg ulcer) is met, but not the usual
% quantity — the LCD's weekly change, 4 a month per wound in the table
% dressing_changes, where the order's twice a week is already 8 or 9 and the
% 160 units billed far more. The model counts the table's changes as units
% of service, where A6456 is billed per yard, so its A6456 limit is lower
% than the LCD means (a candidate correction: the yards per change of a
% bandage). The ulcers of both legs are one wound here (with one per leg
% the limit is 8). The Council's criterion is in the model as a condition
% of payment (section remedy): no fact makes the punctate ulcers a
% qualifying wound, so the claim would fail there even within the quantity
% — and today's LCD says so of A6456 itself: "Claims for A6456 used for
% treatment of venous insufficiency without a qualifying wound ... will be
% denied as statutorily non-covered, no benefit" (a benefit denial, where
% the Council's was one of necessity). That the ulcers were neither surgical
% nor debrided is a negative fact: it is transcribed by stating neither.
% Not payable either: the use of the bandage (primary or secondary), the
% order's date and the delivery are not stated.
scenario kohlls_pharmacy is, as stated in Council decision kohlls pharmacy:
    claim 1 is for the zinc paste bandages,
        confer "zinc paste impregnated bandages (A6456) (surgical dressings)".
    the HCPCS code of the zinc paste bandages is "A6456",
        confer "zinc paste impregnated bandages (A6456) (surgical dressings)".
    the zinc paste bandages is furnished to the beneficiary,
        confer "provided by the appellant to the beneficiary on June 30, 2008".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2008-06-30,
        confer "provided by the appellant to the beneficiary on June 30, 2008".
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months,
        confer "to be applied twice a week for one month to the beneficiary’s bilateral lower extremities".
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 160,
        confer "the amount dispensed was 16 packages of 10 each (equaling 160 units)".
    the zinc paste bandages is applied to the punctate ulcers,
        confer "The doctor prescribed Unna boots (requiring the zinc impregnated dressings)".
    the punctate ulcers is a wound of the beneficiary,
        confer "still had multiple small punctate ulcers".
    the type of the punctate ulcers is venous insufficiency ulcer,
        confer "the surgical dressings for the treatment of punctate venous ulcers".
    the punctate ulcers was evaluated on 2008-06-24,
        confer "on June 24, 2008, the beneficiary’s physician observed".
    the order for the zinc paste bandages is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "The doctor prescribed Unna boots".
    the order for the zinc paste bandages includes description of the item,
        confer "the physician’s order called for the application of an Unna boot".
    the order for the zinc paste bandages includes quantity to be dispensed,
        confer "the physician’s order called for the application of an Unna boot to the beneficiary’s bilateral lower extremities twice a week for one month".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at question"].

% M-11-1177, MP Totalcare/CCS Medical (22 June 2011, own-motion review).
% Kerlix AMD, a sterile gauze impregnated with an antimicrobial (A6266, per
% linear yard), furnished to 34 beneficiaries between 23 October 2009 and 29
% January 2010, used once a day as a secondary or tertiary dressing over
% wounds after debridement, on signed and dated physician orders; the DME
% MACs denied the quantities billed and in most cases downcoded them to 30
% or 31 units a wound (one a day) of non-impregnated roll gauze (A6446, the
% non-elastic conforming bandage); and alginate covers (A6197) for G.Mi. and
% G.V., whose frequency the QIC found unsupported. The ALJ covered whatever
% the physician had specifically ordered.
% Council: reverses — a physician's order does not establish medical
% necessity (no treating physician rule in a Part B supplier's case, CMS
% Ruling 93-1 giving even a Part A physician's opinion no presumptive
% weight; the supplier bears the burden; FDA clearance only makes an item
% eligible for coverage); the records (a prescription,
% the supplier's "Wound Care Physician Written Order", a "Review of
% Systems" form) do not show that the beneficiaries needed an impregnated
% gauze under LCD L11460, nor more dressings (no prior usage, expected
% changes or reasons). A6266 not covered; covered instead at the downcoded
% level, plain roll gauze once a day per wound (A6446); alginate not covered
% beyond 30/31 a wound a month. The supplier liable.
% Transcribed: D.R.'s Kerlix claim (the decision's example of the records,
% "D.R., Exh. 5"), the same gauze as the contractor downcoded it to A6446
% (the Council's alternative), and G.Mi.'s and G.V.'s alginate claims; the
% wound, the orders and the use are those the decision states for all the
% A6266 claims. No quantity or date of service is given. Left out: the other
% 31 A6266 beneficiaries (L.H. is the other example), L.W. (denied by the
% ALJ), L.C. (withdrawn), R.M.'s second date of service.
% Model: D.R.'s A6266 (claim 1): not reasonable and necessary — agrees on
% the outcome: the quantity billed is not stated, and today's LCD gives
% impregnated gauze no criterion but its change "up to once per day"
% ("Coverage is based upon the characteristics of the underlying
% material(s)"); DISAGREES on the ground: within 30 a wound a month the
% model would cover it, having no rule for the Council's ground, that the
% record shows no need of an impregnated rather than a plain gauze. The
% downcoded roll gauze (claim 2): reasonable and necessary — agrees with the
% Council's alternative (a conforming bandage used as a secondary dressing
% over any wound; the LCD sets no usual frequency for it). G.Mi. and G.V.
% (claims 3 and 4): DISAGREES — the Council allows the alginate up to 30/31
% a wound a month, the model none of it: the alginate criteria read a full
% thickness, moderately to highly exudative wound without eschar, which the
% decision does not describe (the Council did not address them), and the
% model decides a claim as a whole, not the units within the usual
% quantity. None payable: the dates, the order's elements and the wound
% evaluations are not stated. Candidate templates: "*a claim* is allowed
% for *a number* units" (a partial allowance), "*a claim* was downcoded to
% *a code*", "the need for an impregnated dressing is documented for *a
% wound*".
scenario m_11_1177 is, as stated in Council decision m 11 1177:
    claim 1 is for the Kerlix gauze of DR,
        confer "Kerlix® dressings to treat and/or prevent wound infection, billed using HCPCS code A6266".
    the HCPCS code of the Kerlix gauze of DR is "A6266",
        confer "Kerlix® dressings to treat and/or prevent wound infection, billed using HCPCS code A6266".
    the Kerlix gauze of DR is furnished to DR,
        confer "furnished to 34 beneficiaries between October 23, 2009, and January 29, 2010".
    the use of the Kerlix gauze of DR is secondary dressing,
        confer "The supplied sterile impregnated gauze was used once per day as secondary or tertiary dressings to cover wounds after debridement".
    the Kerlix gauze of DR is applied to the wound of DR,
        confer "The supplied sterile impregnated gauze was used once per day as secondary or tertiary dressings to cover wounds after debridement".
    the wound of DR is a wound of DR,
        confer "The supplied sterile impregnated gauze was used once per day as secondary or tertiary dressings to cover wounds after debridement".
    the wound of DR has been debrided,
        confer "to cover wounds after debridement".
    the order for the Kerlix gauze of DR is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "a physician signed and dated written orders for the supplies in these cases".
    the order for the Kerlix gauze of DR includes description of the item,
        confer "the treating physician specifically ordered the impregnated dressings".
    claim 2 is for the roll gauze of DR,
        confer "per wound of non-impregnated sterile roll gauze (A6446)".
    the HCPCS code of the roll gauze of DR is "A6446",
        confer "per wound of non-impregnated sterile roll gauze (A6446)".
    the roll gauze of DR is furnished to DR,
        confer "Medicare coverage is available for the dressings allowed at a down-coded level".
    the use of the roll gauze of DR is secondary dressing,
        confer "The supplied sterile impregnated gauze was used once per day as secondary or tertiary dressings to cover wounds after debridement".
    the roll gauze of DR is applied to the wound of DR,
        confer "the records demonstrate that the beneficiaries required plain gauze at a one per day, per wound frequency".
    claim 3 is for the alginate dressings of GMi,
        confer "The claims for beneficiaries G.Mi. and G.V. concern alginate dressings billed using HCPCS code A6197".
    the HCPCS code of the alginate dressings of GMi is "A6197",
        confer "The claims for beneficiaries G.Mi. and G.V. concern alginate dressings billed using HCPCS code A6197".
    the alginate dressings of GMi is furnished to GMi,
        confer "The claims for beneficiaries G.Mi. and G.V. concern alginate dressings billed using HCPCS code A6197".
    claim 4 is for the alginate dressings of GV,
        confer "The claims for beneficiaries G.Mi. and G.V. concern alginate dressings billed using HCPCS code A6197".
    the HCPCS code of the alginate dressings of GV is "A6197",
        confer "The claims for beneficiaries G.Mi. and G.V. concern alginate dressings billed using HCPCS code A6197".
    the alginate dressings of GV is furnished to GV,
        confer "The claims for beneficiaries G.Mi. and G.V. concern alginate dressings billed using HCPCS code A6197".
    rn expects answers ["claim 2 is reasonable and necessary"].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at question"].

% M-15-582, D.L., a beneficiary appellant (11 May 2015, own-motion review).
% 60 OmniPod insulin pods (A9274, external ambulatory insulin delivery
% system, disposable) furnished 10 December 2013 to a beneficiary with
% brittle diabetes after necrotizing pancreatitis and a pancreatic
% debridement (glucose from 35 to 323, severe symptomatic hypoglycemia), on
% his endocrinologist's recommendation. The pod holds the reservoir,
% cannula, infusion set, inserter, motor and power source of a pump and is
% replaced every three days; the Personal Diabetes Manager that programs it
% is coded E0607 (home glucose monitor) and is not claimed. The ALJ covered
% the pods as the disposable supply of an insulin pump (NCD 280.14, LCD
% L27215, Policy Article A47226).
% Council: not covered — CMS's HCPCS assignment (through the PDAC) binds the
% ALJ and the Council; the pod cannot withstand repeated use, so it is not
% durable medical equipment and NCD 280.14 does not apply; Policy Article
% A47226 declares A9274 a disposable drug delivery system that does not meet
% the definition of DME. A benefit-category (statutory) denial: section 1879
% does not apply; the beneficiary liable.
% Model: not covered — agrees, on the Council's ground: A9274 is within the
% external infusion pump policy's scope and in no benefit category (today's
% Article A52507 keeps the exclusion in the same words, the rule
% infusion_benefit), nor is the pod durable medical equipment by the
% Manual's definition (dmepos.le), no fact saying it withstands repeated
% use: the query fails at applicability. The insulin pump criteria of LCD
% L33794 (C-peptide or autoantibodies, the injection regimen, the testing)
% are never reached, and the decision states none of them. The PDM's code
% is recorded: it is the glucose monitor policy's (a home blood glucose
% monitor), which would decide it had it been claimed. The beneficiary's
% diabetes is recorded as documented, no ICD-10 code being given (diabetes
